// The type-check config. `npm run type-check` uses THIS file; Next.js only
// ever reads `tsconfig.json`, and that separation is the whole point.
//
// `@cloud/*` is the build-time route alias (see next.config.mjs). Its real
// mapping lives in webpack's `resolve.alias`, and it must NOT be declared in
// `tsconfig.json` `paths`: Next hands tsconfig paths to webpack through
// `JsConfigPathsPlugin`, which taps enhanced-resolve's `described-resolve`
// hook. Webpack's own `resolve.alias` is an `AliasPlugin` registered on
// `raw-resolve`, and the pipeline runs `described-resolve` -> `raw-resolve`,
// so the tsconfig entry is consulted FIRST and wins. It wins on pipeline
// ordering, which is architectural; NOT on plugin registration order, which
// would be incidental and could change. A `@cloud/*` entry in tsconfig.json
// would therefore OVERRIDE the alias and silently resolve every composed
// cloud build back to the OSS `cloud-absent/` stubs — a build that succeeds
// and serves the wrong thing.
//
// `cloud-route-shims.test.ts` asserts no such entry exists, because every
// other gate is blind to it: tsc reads THIS file, whose `paths` REPLACES the
// base's wholesale (tsc merges `compilerOptions` key by key); vitest declares
// its own `@cloud` alias; and `next build` succeeds either way, with
// `typescript.ignoreBuildErrors` on besides.
//
// Narrower than it first looks, and worth knowing which mistake is the fatal
// one: `JsConfigPathsPlugin` tries its candidates with `forEachBail` and falls
// through when NONE of them exist on disk. So the break needs an entry whose
// first EXISTING candidate is the wrong one — precisely what copying the
// webpack alias (`@cloud/* -> ./src/cloud-absent/*`) produces. Copying the
// composed-first pair below would accidentally behave correctly. Do not rely
// on that.
//
// `tsc` still needs a mapping, so it gets one here, in a file no bundler
// reads. The two candidates are ordered composed-first: TypeScript tries each
// substitution in turn and takes the first that exists on disk, which is
// exactly the switch `cloudControlPresent` performs for webpack. With the
// overlay linked, `@cloud/*` type-checks against cloud-control's real sources
// under this app's `strict` settings (`preserveSymlinks` in the base config
// is what makes their own bare imports resolve); without it, against the
// stubs. Either way both sides of the alias are ordinary paths, so both are
// really checked.
//
// Trade-off, stated rather than hidden: editors read `tsconfig.json`, so an
// IDE reports `@cloud/*` as unresolved in the route shims. They are
// two-line re-exports, `npm run type-check` and CI cover them, and the
// alternative is a paths entry that breaks the composed build.
{
  "extends": "./tsconfig.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "paths": {
      "@/*": ["./src/*"],
      "@cloud/*": [
        "./node_modules/@qontinui/cloud-control/frontend/src/*",
        "./src/cloud-absent/*"
      ]
    }
  }
}
